The '''Arrabád murders''' were a series of killings of [[W:Sex worker|sex workers]] in the [[Arrabád]] barrio of the [[District of Castejón]] between March and October 1887. At least seven women were killed, with three additional deaths from the same period suspected to be linked. The murders were investigated not by the [[Mozos de Escuadra]] — the district police force, which declined to pursue the case — but by the [[Sindicat de Trabajadores de l'Arrabád]], mutual aid society for sex workers, with the support of the communal assemblies and anarchist organizations of the Arrabád.

The investigation identified the killer as [[Francesc Vidal i Ballester]], a prominent Castejón merchant and municipal councillor. The subsequent trial exposed the complicity of the Mozos de Escuadra, who had accepted bribes from Vidal i Ballester to ignore the killings, and the broader network of ''camarillas'' — protection rackets that controlled brothels through debt bondage and violence — that operated with impunity in the Arrabád. The public outrage following the trial was a major catalyst for the [[1888 decriminalization of sex work in Castejón]], which legalized the sale of sex between consenting adults and redirected law enforcement resources toward prosecuting exploitation rather than workers.

== Background ==

{{main|Arrabád|Prostitution in Balisca}}

The [[Arrabád]] — from the Bayani ''rabaḍ'' ({{Literal translation|suburb|outskirts|slums}}) — was (prior to the consolidation of Castejón), an seperate city within the [[District of Castejón]], populated by a mixed Maroon, Mozbayani, and Kaftian neighbourhood that had functioned as a semi-autonomous zone since the Volisanian colonial period. Sex work had operated in the Arrabád for centuries without significant legal interference, but this permissiveness was the tolerance of neglect: the state looked away not because it recognized sex work as legitimate, but because it did not consider the Arrabád's inhabitants worth the trouble of policing.

By the 1880s, the Arrabád's sex trade was controlled by the ''camarillas'' — protection rackets that operated brothels through [[W:Debt bondage|debt bondage]], confiscated workers' earnings, and enforced compliance through violence. The [[W:Criminalization of sex work|criminalization of sex work]] made it impossible for workers to report abuse, because reporting their exploiters meant admitting to a crime. The Mozos de Escuadra were complicit: individual officers accepted bribes from the camarillas, and the force as an institution treated the Arrabád as beyond the scope of district law enforcement.

A concurrent [[W:Syphilis|syphilis]] epidemic had been spreading through the port districts since the early 1880s. The municipal health board's 1887 report — suppressed by the district council until the murders forced its release — estimated that infection rates in the Arrabád were three times the citywide average, and that criminalization was the single greatest obstacle to containment.

== The murders ==

Between March and October 1887, seven sex workers in the Arrabád were found dead — strangled or beaten, their bodies discovered in the courtyards and alleyways of the district. The victims were:

