Three additional deaths from the same period — those of [[Antònia Prats]] (March), [[Elisabet Vila]] (June), and [[Magdalena Soler]] (September) — are suspected to be linked but could not be conclusively attributed due to the state of the remains.

All seven confirmed victims were sex workers associated with the same network of ''camarilla''-controlled brothels. Several had attempted to leave the camarillas' control in the weeks before their deaths, and at least two — Montserrat and Cardona — had been identified by neighbours as having reported abuse to the Mozos de Escuadra, reports that were never acted upon.

== Investigation ==

The Mozos de Escuadra declined to open a formal investigation, citing insufficient evidence and the "transient and disreputable character of the victims." The [[Sindicat de Treballadores de l'Arrabád]], led by [[Dolors Piera i Santmartí]], organized its own investigation. The Sindicat's members canvassed the Arrabád, interviewed witnesses, documented the victims' movements in the hours before their deaths, and identified a pattern: all seven women had been seen with the same man — a well-dressed Volisanian in his fifties — in the hours before their bodies were discovered.

The Sindicat's investigation identified the man as [[Francesc Vidal i Ballester]], a textile merchant and municipal councillor who owned three properties in the Arrabád — including the building at [[Carrer de les Gerànies 14]] where two of the bodies were found. Vidal i Ballester was a client of the kamarillas and had been identified by multiple sex workers as a violent and abusive patron. The investigation further revealed that Vidal i Ballester had been paying individual ''Mozos'' to suppress reports of the killings and to discourage witnesses from cooperating with the Sindicat's inquiry.

The Sindicat presented its findings to the municipal council on 2 November 1887, accompanied by a petition signed by 4,000 residents of the Arrabád demanding Ballester's arrest and a full investigation into the Mozos' complicity. The council, under intense public pressure, ordered the arrest of Ballester on 5 November 1887.

=== Trial ===
The trial of Francesc Vidal i Ballester began on 12 February 1888 and lasted six weeks. The prosecution was led by [[Jordi Casals]], a [[Catharism|Catharist]] lawyer that was known to have sympathetic views towards the urban poor, who presented the Sindicat's evidence alongside testimony from surviving sex workers who had witnessed prior bouts of violence from Ballester. The defence argued that the victims' criminal status — as sex workers operating outside the law — made them unreliable witnesses and that the Sindicat's investigation was [[W:Vigilante justice|vigilante justice]], not evidence.

The trial's turning point came when [[Rosa Montserrat]]'s landlady, [[Antònia Gispert y Saüc]], testified that she had seen Vidal i Ballester entering Montserrat's room on the night of 18 March and leaving before dawn on 19 March — the morning Montserrat's body was discovered. Saüc's testimony was corroborated by two other residents of the building, and the prosecution demonstrated that Ballester's alibi — that he had been at a merchants' dinner that evening — was false; the dinner had been cancelled.

Ballester was found guilty on all seven counts of murder on 26 March 1888 and sentenced to life imprisonment. He was murded by another prisoner during a brawl in 1903. Three Mozos de Escuadra officers were subsequently convicted of corruption and dereliction of duty.

== Aftermath ==

{{main|Prostitution in Balisca}}

The Arrabád murders were the immediate catalyst for the [[1888 Castejón decriminalization of sex work]]. The municipal council voted 14–6 on 12 September 1888 to decriminalize the sale of sex between consenting adults, establish a municipal health inspection programme, and redirect law enforcement resources from prosecuting sex workers to prosecuting pimps and traffickers. The decision's sponsors explicitly cited the murders as evidence that criminalization had failed — that it had not eliminated sex work but had instead created the conditions in which exploitation, violence, and official complicity could flourish unchecked.

The murders also triggered the [[Municipal Inquiry into the Arrabád Killings]] (1888), which documented the camarillas' control of the district's sex trade and the Mozos de Escuadra's systematic corruption. The Inquiry's recommendations formed the basis of the district council's reform programme and were later incorporated into the [[1948 Confederal Sex Workers' Protection Act]].

The Sindicat de Trabajadoras de l'Arrabád continued to operate as a mutual aid society and advocacy organization, and its model of worker self-organization was cited as a precedent when the 1948 Act established the legal framework for sex worker cooperatives. [[Dolors Piera i Santmartí]], the Sindicat's leader during the investigation, became a prominent figure in Castejón's labour movement and was later elected to the district council.

